{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"menu-change-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Change a restaurant menu","description":"Takes a restaurant or cafe through a menu change in gated steps - review the current menu, draft and cost dishes, test and finalise, train and launch, then review after four weeks.","category":"hospitality","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review","design","build","ship"],"role":["operations-manager","manager","founder"],"subject":["hospitality"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["dataset","notes","text"],"output":["plan","table","checklist","report"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["menu-change","seasonal-menu","recipe-costing","menu-launch","staff-tasting","allergens"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["engineer-restaurant-menu","cost-menu-dish","write-dish-spec-sheet","build-allergen-matrix","write-menu-descriptions"],"personas":["hospitality-manager"]},"args":[{"name":"venue","description":"The venue, its concept and guests, kitchen equipment and team, price level, and why the menu is changing (season, cost increases, slow sales, new chef).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"current_menu","description":"The current menu with prices, and sales and costs per dish if you have them.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"launch_date","description":"When the new menu should go live. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"review","file":"steps/01-review-current-menu.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/01-review.md"},{"id":"draft","file":"steps/02-draft-and-cost.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/02-draft-and-costing.md"},{"id":"test","file":"steps/03-test-and-finalise.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/03-final-menu.md"},{"id":"launch","file":"steps/04-train-and-launch.md","stage":"ship","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/04-launch-plan.md"},{"id":"after","file":"steps/05-four-week-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"menu/05-four-week-review.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs a menu change the way an experienced head chef and manager would: decide what to keep and why before writing new dishes, cost every new dish before it is tasted, test it on the real line, train the team and update allergen information before launch, then check the numbers after four weeks. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps use only what was approved.\n\n<venue>\n{{venue}}\n</venue>\n\n<current_menu>\n{{current_menu}}\n</current_menu>\n{{#launch_date}}\n\nLaunch date: {{launch_date}}\n{{/launch_date}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only figures the user gave or confirmed. Mark gaps as `[needed]`; never invent supplier prices, sales or costs.\n- Show arithmetic for every margin and food cost figure.\n- Keep the kitchen's real capacity in view: stations, equipment, prep time and team skills on a busy service.\n- Allergen and dietary information must be updated before any new dish is sold; never state a dish is free from an allergen without checked ingredients and supplier labels.\n- Keep dietary coverage: vegetarian, vegan or other options the venue offers must not disappear without a replacement.\n- Plain words and short tables; each artifact ends with open questions.","steps":[{"id":"review","file":"steps/01-review-current-menu.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/01-review.md","text":"# Step 1: Review the current menu\n\n1. What sells and what earns: for each dish, sales, price net of sales tax, food cost and cash margin where given. Mark missing data.\n2. Keep, change or drop: a first view for each dish with the reason (best seller, high margin, regulars' favourite, slow, low margin, hard to execute, seasonal ingredient ending).\n3. Kitchen load: dishes that overload one station or need long prep for few sales.\n4. Gaps: price points, dietary options, or styles missing for the venue's guests.\n5. The brief for new dishes: how many per section, target price and food cost or cash margin, station and prep limits, seasonal ingredients to use.\n\nSections: Sales and margins, Keep change or drop, Kitchen load, Gaps, Brief for new dishes, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"draft","file":"steps/02-draft-and-cost.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/02-draft-and-costing.md","text":"# Step 2: Draft and cost new dishes\n\n1. Draft each new dish against the approved brief: components, portion sizes, station, prep needed, and how it uses ingredients across the menu to limit waste and new stock lines.\n2. Cost each dish: ingredient quantities, the user's purchase prices, yields (marked as estimates where no yield test exists), portion cost, food cost % at the proposed price, and cash margin. Missing prices stay `[needed]`.\n3. Allergens: list the allergens in each component from the named ingredients and flag bought-in items whose labels must be checked.\n4. Kitchen check: for each station, the new prep and pick-up load compared with the current menu.\n5. A shortlist with one or two back-ups per section.\n\nSections: Draft dishes, Costing, Allergens to check, Kitchen check, Shortlist, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"test","file":"steps/03-test-and-finalise.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/03-final-menu.md","text":"# Step 3: Test and finalise\n\n1. Test plan: cook each shortlisted dish on the real station at service speed, timed from ticket to pass, and taste with the team; a scoring sheet for taste, look, speed and consistency.\n2. Adjust from the user's test notes: recipe, portion or garnish changes, with the cost effect recalculated.\n3. Final menu: dishes per section, prices (net and with tax), and dishes leaving the menu.\n4. Paperwork for each final dish: spec sheet outline (quantities, pick-up steps, plating), updated allergen information to enter into the allergen matrix, and new stock lines and par levels.\n5. Menu descriptions: short, accurate names and descriptions that match what is served.\n\nSections: Test plan, Adjustments, Final menu, Paperwork, Descriptions, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"launch","file":"steps/04-train-and-launch.md","stage":"ship","gate":"approve","artifact":"menu/04-launch-plan.md","text":"# Step 4: Train and launch\n\n1. Launch timeline working back from the launch date: supplier orders, print or digital menu updates, online listings and delivery platforms, allergen matrix updated, stock of old items run down.\n2. Kitchen training: a run-through for each station using the spec sheets, and a check that every cook can produce each dish to spec.\n3. Front of house briefing: a staff tasting, how to describe each dish, pairing or upsell lines that are honest, and how to answer allergy questions using the matrix only.\n4. Launch week: a soft start if possible, what the manager watches each service (ticket times, returns, comments) and how to fix issues fast.\n5. A one-page checklist for the day before launch.\n\nSections: Launch timeline, Kitchen training, Front of house briefing, Launch week, Day-before checklist, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"after","file":"steps/05-four-week-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"menu/05-four-week-review.md","text":"# Step 5: Four-week review\n\n1. Results: sales mix per dish, food cost %, cash margin and average spend compared with the old menu, using the user's figures. Show the arithmetic.\n2. Kitchen and guests: ticket times, waste, returns and recurring review comments.\n3. Decisions: dishes to keep, adjust or replace, with the reason and the margin effect.\n4. Lessons for the next menu change.\n\nSections: Results, Kitchen and guests, Decisions, Lessons."}]}
